[{:db/id "q-37603/selection/544621c35887b70181176d3b9cb60f8904ca3fa36e2d31396bf90e4564e53a81" :answer/id #uuid "a2589e9e-58b4-56f7-b69b-fe4ee1dfde0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37603/selection/479611f4e594daab55809da7508392211f21e6bbf38a07be519528f96c708ba8" :answer/id #uuid "b574bed2-62d7-52f9-90fd-b1de2c5a5bbe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37603/selection/eb354e014ca1de1291e83e4bba7849b5751e691e5c532f579d826f67c280b025" :answer/id #uuid "3f3d8acb-e004-58c7-9a26-f1e80abc1d6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37603/selection/3e6a161b20198143365fbca3e2ba06b0d584ef180e9c0f88e4ddc0d7897cda62" :answer/id #uuid "5a9d049c-1b03-59b1-bff4-cb17edecb534" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37603/selection/a2add130e83c9690e0a6b9ce6c31cba05f99c5325ffb59f0c479d7868b8b389f" :answer/id #uuid "60e1bc71-3ff1-5d10-8761-dd67f4b15941" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 15 \\\\ 4 & 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37603/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fbc2f8cf-3df9-5f33-87c2-1c3e71d74c08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-37603/selection/eb354e014ca1de1291e83e4bba7849b5751e691e5c532f579d826f67c280b025" :answer-field/choices ["q-37603/selection/544621c35887b70181176d3b9cb60f8904ca3fa36e2d31396bf90e4564e53a81" "q-37603/selection/479611f4e594daab55809da7508392211f21e6bbf38a07be519528f96c708ba8" "q-37603/selection/eb354e014ca1de1291e83e4bba7849b5751e691e5c532f579d826f67c280b025" "q-37603/selection/3e6a161b20198143365fbca3e2ba06b0d584ef180e9c0f88e4ddc0d7897cda62" "q-37603/selection/a2add130e83c9690e0a6b9ce6c31cba05f99c5325ffb59f0c479d7868b8b389f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-37603" :question/id #uuid "770a3bd2-95ed-564f-acba-14bf194a130c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37603" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $A$, then  $A$ must be diagonalizable. \n\nSo, we can write $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $A$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 15 \\\\ 4 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-37603/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37603"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-37271"] :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $C$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $C,$ then $C$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $C=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $C,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nC={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}](-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 15 \\\\ 20 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-37597"] :question/problem "Find ${A}^{97},$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have been given the following diagonalization of $A.$\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{97} & =P{D}^{97}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{97}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{97} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{97}\\end{aligned}](-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-40138/selection/b5de113dfd3dcd50af9927def795de7ea53a48c50a614ad6699d2d676f49fb14" :answer/id #uuid "97161230-27e5-5dd7-b345-2cbdaec609e9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40138/selection/669f52ef3827343346c385f6a5327a471731599334321211d8308aedcfc759fb" :answer/id #uuid "ea255ed7-1a12-5cb0-a923-774891ff63fa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40138/selection/e564f6ba28098c1ffb0f9617eb11dc9ae5c21ac236fae104af7ab65be5b6a35d" :answer/id #uuid "edb32f80-7028-50d3-affd-1f8653c85b06" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40138/selection/71b124163d48fd8142759cb53ee4395127638059cff597b05b9b3c3d8960971b" :answer/id #uuid "36137546-3c9b-55a8-a637-1cbada7bf34f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40138/selection/7a89e73b785588dc635f24c5f7e414b663d3b55abd9524a8e8a08c20d0ba22e4" :answer/id #uuid "00adcd49-eae6-5ac5-81c7-7bc8c45f0316" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40138/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "479a086e-d625-5964-aae8-f9b077cfc523" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-40138/selection/7a89e73b785588dc635f24c5f7e414b663d3b55abd9524a8e8a08c20d0ba22e4" :answer-field/choices ["q-40138/selection/b5de113dfd3dcd50af9927def795de7ea53a48c50a614ad6699d2d676f49fb14" "q-40138/selection/669f52ef3827343346c385f6a5327a471731599334321211d8308aedcfc759fb" "q-40138/selection/e564f6ba28098c1ffb0f9617eb11dc9ae5c21ac236fae104af7ab65be5b6a35d" "q-40138/selection/71b124163d48fd8142759cb53ee4395127638059cff597b05b9b3c3d8960971b" "q-40138/selection/7a89e73b785588dc635f24c5f7e414b663d3b55abd9524a8e8a08c20d0ba22e4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-40138" :question/id #uuid "82160b99-37f5-52f2-85ae-e1d38b048f40" :question/math-academy-id "q-40138" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ given that \n\n$$\nB[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$ \n\n$$\nB={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-40138/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-40138"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7133"] :question/problem "Find a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $C$ that has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=1$ and $\\lambda_2=-4,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/assets/f478b4206f3a8bcc5c025fa0c58bb75430bcb1493c1d8470366864f7fb7ceb6e.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathcal{B}= \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $C,$ then $C$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $C=PDP^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $C,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/assets/1cf1ba5d93618341227836a110479ebfc258894f9d4f60ae4df1be682d7c74fc.png)\n\nLet ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/assets/f1defa7b451fcd56632030e9c3663ad99646c5eabc98eb4e1c5b687687b9fdc4.png) Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5502"] :question/problem "Find $A^{100},$ where \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/assets/66521b0220a4a850308d42aeccf17ce4769e563eeec6a7e1870adbcb0f46a318.png)" :question/worked-solution "First,  notice that\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/assets/9c5de663d2cf109c4faaf839760b0afac6dcbf5ab533570100167727853cb74f.png)\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/assets/3f73ff47f00cf345f0ac0cffd41dc325faf00c9f1d1c0ffe4ad7228e83fef3fa.png)\n\nSince $A=PDP^{-1},$ we can compute $A^k$ by using the formula \n\n$$\nA^{k} = PD^{k}P^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{100} & =P{D}^{100}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{100}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{1}^{100} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {0}^{100}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5503"] :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2 \\times 2$ matrix $E,$ given that \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/assets/3389ba82baf25977d7b8deb9609435dce5a543a863f7d8f98102b45d770b465b.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $E\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/assets/9dee992fa1cc903eb23299b52080a7ebba31ef550bb22c4938c45939e8541be4.png)\n\nTherefore, $E$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $E$ are $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=6$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/assets/23dd5ab30b31bf85d84f290fdf9c71529cfd753b98688149f103980894ad91c6.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
